ai-digest Repository Version History
====================================

v1.2.1 (Release Date: TBD)
--------------------------
- Add .gitattributes. The Markdown documents take diff=markdown, so that a
  diff hunk header names the section it falls in; doc/POLICY and doc/LICENSE
  also take linguist-language=Markdown for highlighting on GitHub. doc/VERSIONS
  is left out because it is underlined plain text, and diff=markdown empties
  the hunk headers that otherwise name the version. doc/COPYING and
  doc/COPYING.LESSER are left out as the licence texts. Each path is named on
  its own rather than matched by a glob, which would catch the '#' comment
  lines of the scripts and the configuration files.
- State the document file naming and attribute rules in doc/POLICY: a Markdown
  document takes .md, the fixed names under doc/ keep no extension because they
  are the names this repository shares with the author's other repositories,
  and an existing document is not renamed for its extension, because its path
  is a public URL that pages outside the repository link to.

v1.2 (2026-08-05)
-----------------
- Name the endpoint settings after the summarization stage instead of
  after a vendor. ANTHROPIC_* and OPENAI_* described neither what they
  addressed nor who answered: an 'anthropic-compatible' endpoint is
  regularly operated by neither Anthropic nor OpenAI, and one set of
  settings per backend meant the OpenAI path drew its retry budget from
  a variable named for the other one. They become a single SUMMARIZER_*
  set - SUMMARIZER_API_KEY, SUMMARIZER_AUTH_TOKEN, SUMMARIZER_BASE_URL,
  SUMMARIZER_MODEL, SUMMARIZER_MAX_RETRIES, SUMMARIZER_THINKING_MODE,
  SUMMARIZER_TOOL_CHOICE_MODE and SUMMARIZER_TEXT_JSON_FALLBACK -
  because one endpoint answers at a time. SUMMARIZER_BACKEND now names
  the wire protocol, 'anthropic-compatible' or 'openai-compatible'
  rather than 'claude' or 'openai', and the command line options follow
  the variables they override.
- Refuse the superseded names at startup rather than reading them.
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN and ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL are
  also what the Anthropic SDK and other tools take from the environment
  on their own, so a value exported for one of those used to decide
  where a nightly digest was sent. Presence is what is refused, not the
  value, and the message names the replacement; a superseded
  SUMMARIZER_BACKEND value is answered the same way. Upgrading a host
  therefore means unsetting the old names wherever they were exported,
  not only editing .env.
- Bound the summarization request with a timeout of its own,
  SUMMARIZER_TIMEOUT, defaulting to 180 seconds. Neither API client was
  given one, so both fell back on an SDK default measured in minutes
  and an endpoint that accepted a request but never answered could hold
  a nightly run open until the next one started. The value is checked
  before anything is collected, and each retry spends it again, so the
  worst case wait is SUMMARIZER_TIMEOUT x (SUMMARIZER_MAX_RETRIES + 1).
  HTTP_TIMEOUT keeps its own meaning and now covers only the collectors
  and the scraper, whose requests are many and short.
- Raise the defaults of MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS to 8000 and HTTP_TIMEOUT to
  60 seconds, and move the viewer from port 5000 to 3000. A deployment
  pinning the old values in .env is unaffected; one relying on the
  defaults answers on the new port after an upgrade.
- Document USER_AGENT in .env.example, which was the one setting
  present in config.py and in the README but missing from the file
  meant to be copied.
- Add doc/DEPLOYMENT.md and deploy/, covering the Debian and nginx
  procedure end to end: installation, the batch under cron, TLS, reader
  restrictions, the API compatibility settings, the request budget, and
  the routine operations the README never stated - rotating the key,
  rotating the batch log, backing up the archive and rolling back. The
  deployment section of the README now points at it instead of carrying
  a shortened copy of the same unit file.
- Say in the README what a failure looks like: the exit codes, the log
  line each failure writes and what it usually means, the shared log
  format, and that no credential appears at any level. Add the guide
  for adding a collector that the contribution section was inviting
  without describing, and the reason the four places carrying Japanese
  carry it.
- List doc/POLICY and doc/VERSIONS in the repository structure and link
  them from the top of the README. Both existed and neither was
  reachable from the document a reader starts at.

v1.1.1 (2026-08-04)
-------------------
- Read the feed timestamps as UTC, which is what feedparser returns.
  time.mktime() read them as local time, so on a host east of UTC every
  entry looked older than it was and the look back window silently lost
  the difference; in Asia/Tokyo a run covered 15 hours instead of 24.
- Name the configured look back window in the prompt and in the summary
  image instead of always announcing 24 hours, so that LOOKBACK_HOURS
  and what the report says about itself cannot disagree.
- Say why a run collected nothing. The collectors now return the per
  source outcome next to their entries, so an empty run is reported as
  unreachable sources, as sources that answered with nothing published
  inside the window, or as no source configured, instead of one message
  that listed every possible cause at once. A run that loses only some
  of its sources continues and says so.
- Copy the composite image of the captured report into doc/screenshots
  from tools/capture_screens.py, so that refreshing the images embedded
  in the README is one command instead of four captures plus a manual
  copy of summary.png.
- Accept every setting as a command line option on cli.py, each named
  after the variable it replaces, so that a one off run states what it
  wants, as in 'cli.py run --lookback-hours 72', instead of needing the
  environment edited or a variable prefixed. An option left out changes
  nothing, so an existing cron entry behaves as before; the values a run
  replaced are logged, and a number the pipeline cannot use is refused
  by the parser before anything is collected. The three credentials
  deliberately have no option, because a command line is readable by
  every user of the host.
- Keep the categories of the leading topics in the legend of the
  summary image. The legend is right aligned and was drawn from the
  right until the row was full, so a day with long category names lost
  the entries of its most important topics rather than of its least
  important ones.
- Draw a card instead of ending the run when an image is refused as a
  decompression bomb. Such a picture compresses to far less than the
  byte limit stops, and Pillow reports it outside OSError, so the error
  escaped the resolver and ended the batch with a traceback after the
  summarization had already been paid for.
- Record the look back window a run collected in the statistics of its
  report, so that 'cli.py render' redraws the summary image with the
  period the report was built from instead of whatever LOOKBACK_HOURS
  holds at the time of the rebuild.
- Fall back on the default identity when USER_AGENT is set but empty,
  and ignore the space around it. Every other string setting already
  reads an empty value as unset, so a line reading 'USER_AGENT=' in
  .env was the one way to send an empty header to arXiv and to the
  feed and publisher hosts, which are free to refuse it.
- Apply the line limit of wrap_text() to an explicit line break as
  well. A break added a line without counting it, so a text carrying
  one could be given more lines than the card reserves room for and
  run over the illustration below it. Nothing produces such a text
  today, since titles and bullets have their whitespace collapsed
  before they are drawn, and the limit now holds whatever arrives.

v1.1 (2026-08-02)
-----------------
- Stop the batch on an unknown SUMMARIZER_BACKEND value instead of
  falling back on the default, which turned a typo into a run against
  the Claude API when an API-key-free run was intended, and remove the
  unused Config.require_api_key() left beside that check.
- Accept only http and https links from the collectors, and neutralize
  the ones already stored when a report is rendered, so that a crafted
  feed entry cannot place a javascript: URL in the report.
- Enforce the image size limit while the response is being read rather
  than after the whole body has been buffered, and request image
  sources over http and https only.
- Anchor the report date pattern with \A and \Z, since $ also matched
  in front of a trailing newline.
- Cover the configuration, the report paths, the link filtering, the
  capped download and the plain backend with unit tests.
- Add ANTHROPIC_THINKING_MODE and ANTHROPIC_TOOL_CHOICE_MODE, which
  turn the thinking output off and let the model pick the tool, for an
  Anthropic-compatible endpoint that accepts the request but answers
  with thinking alone until the output budget is gone and so never
  returns a tool_use block. The defaults leave the request Anthropic
  receives unchanged, and an unknown value stops the run before
  anything is collected.
- Add 'any' to ANTHROPIC_TOOL_CHOICE_MODE, which demands a tool without
  naming one, for an endpoint that drops a named tool_choice but
  honours an unnamed demand; build_report being the only tool offered,
  both reach the same place.
- Add ANTHROPIC_TEXT_JSON_FALLBACK, which reads a report an endpoint
  wrote as JSON text rather than as a tool call. It is off by default
  and accepts only a text block parsing into an object with a topics
  list, so that prose is never published as a report, and it names
  itself in a warning whenever it is used.
- Read MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS from the environment. It was a constant in the
  summarizer, so a value placed in .env was loaded and then ignored,
  while the message a truncated report prints told the reader to raise
  exactly that name. Both API backends take the budget from the
  configuration now, and a value below 1 stops the run before anything
  is collected.
- Add ANTHROPIC_MAX_RETRIES, so that a run can be made to spend exactly
  one request while two endpoint settings are compared, without
  editing the summarizer as the earlier investigation did.
- Add SUMMARIZER_BACKEND=openai, an OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions
  path for a provider whose Anthropic-compatible endpoint returns no
  usable tool call. It sends the same prompt and the same tool schema,
  reads the answer from tool_calls[].function.arguments and validates
  it through the same to_topics(), so a report does not differ by the
  route it took. The openai package stays out of requirements.txt and
  is imported only when that backend runs.
- Name the stop reason and the content block types when the summarizer
  finds no build_report call, report a truncated or unparsable set of
  arguments as such instead of letting a partial report through as an
  empty one, accept only a block named build_report instead of the
  first tool call seen, log one summary line per response instead of
  the whole body, and restore the default client retries that the
  earlier investigation had disabled.
- Add doc/POLICY, the ai-digest implementation policy adapted for a
  single Python project from the shared id774/scripts policy.

v1.0 (2026-07-31)
-----------------
- Add the 'demo' command, which builds a report from the sample bundled
  in ai_digest/demo without collecting anything or calling the API, and
  document the output with screenshots captured from it by
  tools/capture_screens.py.
- Add SUMMARIZER_BACKEND=plain, letting the batch pipeline run without
  an Anthropic API key by building topics mechanically instead of
  calling the Claude API.
- Support custom Anthropic-compatible API URLs and Bearer authentication.

